@startuml
!include style/ail.puml
title G6 · Store states: who is responsible for destruction?
top to bottom direction
skinparam roundcorner 18
hide empty description
state "No owned store\nProgram has nothing to destroy" as absent
state "Live Store\nProgram is responsible for destroying it" as live
state "destroy is running\nLibrary has taken responsibility" as destroying
state "Store is destroyed\nLibrary has discharged the obligation\nProgram can no longer use its borrowed views" as dead
absent -[bold]-> live : create returns a store\nand gives ownership to the program
absent -[bold]-> absent : create returns null\nProgram has nothing to destroy
live -[bold]-> live : view lends access to bytes\nProgram keeps responsibility
live -[bold]-> destroying : Program enters destroy\nand transfers responsibility
destroying -[bold]-> dead : Library destroys Store\nbefore destroy returns
legend bottom
  Rounded box = resource/obligation state; bold arrow = labelled state transition.
  A loop returns to the same responsibility. Arrows follow states, not call messages.
  Borrowing lets the program use bytes without adding another destruction obligation.
  Non-normative: C2, C3, C5; synthetic buffers example only.
  This view follows one unique store. Reference counting and the separate
  prepare/read_prepared ordering example are outside its scope.
endlegend
@enduml
